A||N||4||glava2876||84 4 cyl. questions||||5||
Z||000000||glava2876||04-16-2001||09:51 PM||||I have been working on my Fiero, 37k miles, but sat indoors for 10 years, and I'm close to firing it up. I put Wd-40 in each cyl., drygas/ carb cleaner and fresh gas. Charging the battery. I checked the oil and it really didn't look bad at all -it was changed about 50 miles before storage. In general,it sounds like Fieros with this motor may have had problems -should I run synthetic oil? How about in the transaxle? What simple performance modifications would everyone recommend? Thanks, Glen  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-16-2001||10:05 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!<P>One of the best things that you can do is just to a complete tune up.  New plugs, wires, distributor cap.  Change your air/fuel/oil filter and change the oil.  As for synthetic oil, it is mostly a matter of preference.  I run regular oil and it still runs great for a 220,000 mile engine.<P>Transaxle oil:  what tranny do you have?  standard or auto?<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||04-16-2001||10:23 PM||||If you have a manual transaxle I would strongly recommend using the GM Syncromesh transmission fluid.  It is expensive but it has made a noticeable improvement in shifting smoothness in my manual Fieros.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||04-16-2001||10:31 PM||||Hey Glen!<BR>I have over 250K miles on an 84...<BR>There have been 3 transplants in all that time, I am rough on engines!<BR>If you have an auto trans, then Dextron II...<BR>A manual uses 5W30 motor oil...<BR>Get a set of Rapid fire plugs and an Accell coil and do a tune up...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-16-2001||10:53 PM||||If accell's regular coils have dropped to the same extremely poor quality I've seen in their DIS coils.... I'll go someplace else.<P>Change that oil.<P>spray the cylinders again. or give them a around a 1/2 ounce of marvel mystery oil. then turn the engine over at least twice with a wrench AFTER you drian the oil so you don't pick any of that old trash up. If you can't turn it over with a wrench then the starter will likely break something.<P>Then put on a filter and fill up the oil. then pull the coil primary connector so it WILL NOT start. crank it untill you see the oil pressure come up. (If it doesn't come up while cranking a few seconds watch the gauge as you let go of the key. I'm not sure if the gauge reads durring cranking on these. I haven't paid much attention to it.)<P>Once you are reasonably sure you have oil pressure and a free spinning motor THEN worry about starting it.<P>BTW, I do mean the primay wire on the coil. Don't pull the heavy secondary from it or the dist. You don't want the coil to fry something with energy that can't get to the plugs.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-16-2001).]||||reg||1
